Let's start with, maybe tell us a little bit, what are the roles of stun and turn servers, the differences, what makes them critical in WebRTC?

42
00:03:37,902 --> 00:03:41,790
I think there are two things that make stun and turn necessary.

43
00:03:42,254 --> 00:03:45,330
One of them is the fact WebRTC is peer-to-peer.

44
00:03:46,734 --> 00:03:47,990
In a second, we'll go over that.

45
00:03:47,990 --> 00:03:53,330
And the second is WebRTC is running over UDP by default.

46
00:03:55,150 --> 00:03:56,750
Let's start with the peer-to-peer thing.

47
00:03:57,310 --> 00:04:01,490
WebRTC is peer-to-peer, which means that I can take two browsers and connect them to each other.

48
00:04:02,606 --> 00:04:04,770
The only problem is that they usually don't have a direct

49
00:04:04,770 --> 00:04:06,530
path from one to the other.

50
00:04:06,670 --> 00:04:12,590
So you can't really connect them because you don't know their IP addresses, which makes it a problem.

51
00:04:13,710 --> 00:04:16,250
In many cases, though, the service isn't peer-to-peer.

52
00:04:16,250 --> 00:04:22,970
If we take, for example, where we're running this or recording this session, it uses an SFU, a media server.

53
00:04:23,170 --> 00:04:24,650
So we're not running peer-to-peer.

54
00:04:24,770 --> 00:04:25,910
I'm not sending my media to you.

55
00:04:25,950 --> 00:04:31,268
I'm sending that to the media server that negotiates everything on my behalf.

56
00:04:33,166 --> 00:04:38,630
There, I might still need a turn server because UDP might be blocked and I can't reach that server directly.

57
00:04:39,246 --> 00:04:40,770
So I need to go indirectly to it.

58
00:04:41,742 --> 00:04:43,748
Okay, so that would be the reason why we need it.

59
00:04:44,782 --> 00:04:49,970
For that, we've got two different protocols in a way that happen to be two different servers.

60
00:04:50,170 --> 00:04:52,150
One of them is TAN and the other is TURN.

61
00:04:53,294 --> 00:04:57,750
As we said, we've got two IP addresses, this one and this one, both of two different users.

62
00:04:58,478 --> 00:05:01,010
They both know their local IP address.

63
00:05:01,310 --> 00:05:02,050
What does that mean?

64
00:05:02,830 --> 00:05:09,270
If I sit here in my home, I've got my own local private network and my home private IP address.

65
00:05:09,870 --> 00:05:16,790
If I need to go toward the internet, I need to use a public IP address to reach a public web service.

66
00:05:17,454 --> 00:05:23,690
If I want to reach out to your website or to you directly, I will need your public IP address as well.

67
00:05:24,878 --> 00:05:25,630
Now, here's the thing.

68
00:05:25,750 --> 00:05:30,230
I have no clue what my public IP address is, and neither the device.

69
00:05:31,118 --> 00:05:35,950
The device needs to kind of figure that out, and the way to do that is through a STAN server.

70
00:05:36,878 --> 00:05:45,990
What happens is that I'm going to send a message, WebRTC will send a message from the client to the STAN server, asking him, hey, what's my IP address?

71
00:05:46,542 --> 00:05:47,110
What do you see?

72
00:05:48,110 --> 00:05:49,390
The server receives the address.

73
00:05:49,530 --> 00:05:59,570
In order to do that, we went through a NAT device, a Network Address Translator device on the network, and that device is the one that allocates the public IP address for me.

74
00:06:00,718 --> 00:06:10,468
Since we go through that, the server sees that address, the STAN server, and then it would simply reply back to me saying, well, that's the IP address that I see, that's your public IP address.

75
00:06:11,918 --> 00:06:14,450
So that's the only reason we need a STUN server.

76
00:06:15,022 --> 00:06:24,610
Once I've got this public IP address, theoretically, I can give that address to someone else, for example, to you, and you can send me that directly to me.

77
00:06:25,678 --> 00:06:28,388
Sometimes it works, sometimes it doesn't.

78
00:06:29,198 --> 00:06:40,390
It doesn't when the NAT address translator, the NAT device decides that it wants to be very, very strict as to whom, who can send me data when I send that message to the STAN server.

79
00:06:40,530 --> 00:06:49,730
It might simply say, well, it's nice of you to send out data, but only that STAN server can send back data through that pinhole that you created.

80
00:06:51,022 --> 00:06:59,070
So there are restrictions to this that might take place, that might occur, which is why we need It's something like TURN, okay?

81
00:06:59,726 --> 00:07:03,300
And like STAN, TURN simply says, I'm going to relay everything on your behalf.

82
00:07:04,238 --> 00:07:08,370
Whatever you want to send to Aaron, you're going to send to me as your TURN server.

83
00:07:08,718 --> 00:07:14,130
I'm sitting in the cloud in a public IP address, so I'm reachable by both of you guys.

84
00:07:15,050 --> 00:07:17,950
If you send me the data, I'll just relay that to Aaron.

85
00:07:19,086 --> 00:07:19,390
Okay?

86
00:07:20,142 --> 00:07:21,250
And that's what TURN does.

87
00:07:22,478 --> 00:07:25,850
The best thing about TURN, it's end-to-end encrypted.

88
00:07:25,850 --> 00:07:32,050
It doesn't know what goes on inside the relay, the actual media, it just sends it.

89
00:07:32,494 --> 00:07:37,530
And only me and you can actually encrypt and decrypt the data over there, over that specific connection.

90
00:07:38,446 --> 00:07:42,670
So there is no such thing as saying, well, let's record everything through the TURN server.

91
00:07:43,438 --> 00:07:45,730
You can, but you can't really play that back.

92
00:07:45,870 --> 00:07:46,410
So it's useless.

93
00:07:48,654 --> 00:07:55,260
Now, to complicate things a bit further, there are three types of TURN addresses in a way.

94
00:07:55,470 --> 00:07:57,720
So there is UDP, TCP, and TLS.

95
00:07:58,830 --> 00:07:59,760
Why do we need UDP?

96
00:07:59,960 --> 00:08:00,940
Because we love UDP.

97
00:08:01,460 --> 00:08:03,080
UDP works best with WebRTC.

98
00:08:03,340 --> 00:08:04,580
We've done a session about that.

99
00:08:05,440 --> 00:08:15,236
Live streaming and live media prefers being done over UDP, because that means less retransmissions and better quality at the end of the day on most networks.

100
00:08:16,590 --> 00:08:19,060
Why do we decide that we need also turn TCP?

101
00:08:20,174 --> 00:08:21,440
Because UDP might fail.

102
00:08:22,030 --> 00:08:25,160
and when UDP fails, we want to succeed in some other way.

103
00:08:25,300 --> 00:08:33,539
It might fail because many IT admins would simply go into enterprise network and configure UDP to be blocked.

104
00:08:33,679 --> 00:08:33,840
Why?

105
00:08:33,940 --> 00:08:35,480
Because nobody cares about UDP.

106
00:08:35,822 --> 00:08:46,320
Today that's, I think that's changing because of HTTP version three, but up until now, at least UDP was popularly blocked by many different sites.

107
00:08:46,420 --> 00:08:50,780
And then we say, well, if we can't do things over UDP, let's do them over TCP as a follow-up.

108
00:08:52,046 --> 00:08:52,700
Why TLS?

109
00:08:53,902 --> 00:08:59,460
Because TCP might be blocked due to DPI, Deep Packet Inspection, and then we can use TLS instead.

110
00:09:00,500 --> 00:09:01,520
Why TLS fails?

111
00:09:01,720 --> 00:09:04,720
Because things might fail still, okay?

112
00:09:05,800 --> 00:09:09,280
So we get this kind of a waterfall of things that we want to do.

113
00:09:10,446 --> 00:09:15,580
And the question is, okay, we've got all of these different types and solutions and ways to do things.

114
00:09:16,782 --> 00:09:18,680
which one am I going to use for this call?

115
00:09:19,630 --> 00:09:21,600
For the one that we're doing now, me and you, Aaron.

116
00:09:21,780 --> 00:09:25,280
And the answer for that is I have no clue.

117
00:09:26,120 --> 00:09:26,940
I just don't know.

118
00:09:27,310 --> 00:09:34,180
Which means that I have no, I don't know as WebRTC, as the actual client of the application.

119
00:09:34,894 --> 00:09:38,116
Which means that what we're going to do is we're going to try them out, all of them.

120
00:09:38,670 --> 00:09:41,360
And the way to do that is for another protocol called ICE.

121
00:09:42,094 --> 00:09:44,020
So you've got ICE, STUN, and TURN.

122
00:09:44,622 --> 00:09:49,140
ICE is a protocol, STAN and TURN are both protocols and servers.

123
00:09:49,454 --> 00:09:51,960
So you're going to refer to them as STAN server and TURN server.

124
00:09:52,526 --> 00:09:55,940
When someone says ICE server, it's a mistake, but it usually means TURN.

125
00:09:59,310 --> 00:10:13,920
So ICE is going to take all of the addresses from all of these STAN servers, TURN servers, whatnot, and then try doing the connectivity checks, check the priorities of what we want, and what do we prefer to get connected through.

126
00:10:14,670 --> 00:10:18,820
And then based on that, we'll get to a point where we can connect the session itself.

127
00:10:20,398 --> 00:10:26,120
All of that is automatically done by WebRTC in the background for you when you use it.

128
00:10:27,566 --> 00:10:35,980
Now, if you put on the slides that I prepared, it shows what happens when we make the decision or when WebRTC makes the decision.

129
00:10:36,878 --> 00:10:40,880
So if we're on a peer-to-peer session, Not through a media server, peer-to-peer.

130
00:10:41,934 --> 00:10:45,180
The best thing for us to happen is to do direct UDP traffic.

131
00:10:46,222 --> 00:10:48,560
Peer-to-peer, I'm going to send the data directly to Aaron.

132
00:10:48,580 --> 00:10:49,640
I don't need anyone else.

133
00:10:49,820 --> 00:10:59,220
Maybe I would go to a stan server, understand my public IP address, share that with Aaron, Aaron send the media to me, and that would be the end of it.

134
00:11:00,238 --> 00:11:03,300
So the stan server is there for a blip, and that's the end of it.

135
00:11:04,014 --> 00:11:07,140
Usually I can also find free stan servers out there to use.

136
00:11:07,150 --> 00:11:09,880
the best one or the most popular one is probably the Google one.

137
00:11:10,766 --> 00:11:12,180
I still wouldn't use that.

138
00:11:14,180 --> 00:11:17,660
The reason I won't use that is because usually we need turn servers.

139
00:11:18,100 --> 00:11:24,100
And if we need turn servers, a turn server can also second as a stand server without even saying that.

140
00:11:24,270 --> 00:11:25,480
That's common nature.

141
00:11:26,190 --> 00:11:32,620
When you go to a turn server and tell him, like, look, it's me, please allocate an address on my behalf and relay data.

142
00:11:33,198 --> 00:11:34,620
It would simply say, but you know what?

143
00:11:34,620 --> 00:11:36,500
I saw that public IP address.

144
00:11:37,400 --> 00:11:38,980
So that's kind of a standard for us.

145
00:11:40,814 --> 00:11:43,680
Now, what happens if direct UDP doesn't work?

146
00:11:44,910 --> 00:11:48,960
Next up our wishlist would be relay UDP traffic.

147
00:11:49,200 --> 00:11:51,908
This means going through a turn UDP service.

148
00:11:52,494 --> 00:11:56,040
So I'm going through the turn server, but going to try to communicate via UDP.

149
00:11:57,518 --> 00:12:03,140
If that doesn't work, then we're going to fall back to relaying through TCP, turn TCP.

150
00:12:04,238 --> 00:12:07,020
And if that doesn't work, we're going to do relay TLS.

151
00:12:08,302 --> 00:12:13,120
Now the difference between relay and direct is that a relay adds a hop.

152
00:12:13,240 --> 00:12:18,140
I'm not going direct to Aaron, I'm going to another server and that sends the data and that adds complexity.

153
00:12:19,118 --> 00:12:22,140
Between UDP and TCP, I might lose quality.

154
00:12:22,990 --> 00:12:29,540
Because again, if there are packet losses, UDP actually handles that better for real-time communications.

155
00:12:30,000 --> 00:12:37,120
WebRTC can make more out of UDP than it can on TCP to maintain quality, so we'd rather have GDP over TCP.

156
00:12:37,422 --> 00:12:39,840
TCP versus TLS, I don't care.

157
00:12:40,750 --> 00:12:42,080
They're both almost the same.

158
00:12:42,200 --> 00:12:44,540
TLS adds encryption, but guess what?

159
00:12:44,580 --> 00:12:46,700
We're already encrypting, so we don't care.

160
00:12:47,534 --> 00:12:50,680
It's just there because it might help us move better.

161
00:12:52,462 --> 00:12:56,720
The other thing that we have is, well, there is something called direct TCP traffic.

162
00:12:58,222 --> 00:13:05,360
Some media servers include a solution called ICE-TCP, which allows you to send data over TCP directly to the server.

163
00:13:06,606 --> 00:13:07,580
Now, think about that.

164
00:13:08,430 --> 00:13:09,300
We had two problems.

165
00:13:09,460 --> 00:13:10,960
One is the fact that we're peer-to-peer.

166
00:13:11,100 --> 00:13:13,280
If we're using a media server, we're not peer-to-peer.

167
00:13:13,870 --> 00:13:15,480
The other was this is over UDP.

168
00:13:16,590 --> 00:13:33,340
But with iStcp, we can actually do TCP directly in front of the server, which means that if we are using media servers in our deployment, there is a way to reduce the reliance on turn even further by introducing Ice-TCP on our media servers.

169
00:13:36,302 --> 00:13:44,260
I guess that's it about kind of the basics of where we're at with Tern and a bit of Stan and Ice.

170
00:13:45,518 --> 00:13:45,800
Perfect.

171
00:13:46,414 --> 00:13:47,060
Thank you, Sahi.

172
00:13:48,080 --> 00:13:53,280
You've condensed decades of networking knowledge into just a few minutes there.

173
00:13:53,440 --> 00:13:54,860
So I appreciate it.

174
00:13:56,046 --> 00:14:10,740
Now, obviously we would generally prefer for a variety of cost and complication reasons that traffic is not going over turn, but a certain percentage of sessions do go over turn in at least most configurations.

175
00:14:11,080 --> 00:14:17,460
So what percentage of WebRTC calls in production end up relaying through turn and why?

176
00:14:18,638 --> 00:14:20,800
Well, the sad truth is it depends.

177
00:14:23,918 --> 00:14:27,310
You can't know until you try it out, and I'll give an example, okay?

178
00:14:27,590 --> 00:14:30,790
let's say that you're building a call center.

179
00:14:31,950 --> 00:14:35,690
And in that call center, the ones using WebRTC are the call center agents.

180
00:14:37,454 --> 00:14:43,570
Call center agent means that they are controlled and owned by you as the service provider.

181
00:14:43,810 --> 00:14:45,590
You're the contact center manager.

182
00:14:46,370 --> 00:14:54,270
And you're going to simply say, well, it either runs from the office and then I'm going to configure that to work with UDP because I want my calls to have the best quality.

183
00:14:55,214 --> 00:15:00,510
Or if I'm going to send the agent some, I'm going to tell them, well, you can't work your shifts if it's not going over UDP.

184
00:15:01,774 --> 00:15:08,670
Which is why in a lot of the call center solutions out there, you will see that they don't configure turn servers at all.

185
00:15:09,358 --> 00:15:11,908
They just use UDP and if not, that's luck.

186
00:15:12,942 --> 00:15:15,470
Same happens for many of the cloud gaming platforms.

187
00:15:16,590 --> 00:15:17,490
You want to play a game?

188
00:15:17,670 --> 00:15:18,170
That's great.

189
00:15:18,290 --> 00:15:19,850
You need to have the best network possible.

190
00:15:19,990 --> 00:15:22,050
Otherwise, go find something else to do.

191
00:15:23,430 --> 00:15:23,590
Okay.

192
00:15:25,774 --> 00:15:28,750
Then there are the enterprise guys.

193
00:15:29,934 --> 00:15:38,150
In different enterprise networks and certain cellular networks you can get to 50% or more calls mandating to go through turn servers.

194
00:15:38,350 --> 00:15:38,490
Why?

195
00:15:38,610 --> 00:15:39,210
Just because.

196
00:15:40,654 --> 00:15:46,870
Someone decided the network needs to be restricted and then you need return server in order to traverse that and no other way to go.

197
00:15:49,326 --> 00:15:58,010
Usually it would be said that if you're doing a general purpose or social type of an experience and anywhere between 10 to 20% of the calls would go over return.

198
00:15:59,022 --> 00:16:04,190
And then out of that 5% or less of return TCP and even less than that overturn TLS.

199
00:16:06,222 --> 00:16:07,370
What does that mean for you?

200
00:16:07,450 --> 00:16:08,270
I have no clue.

201
00:16:08,718 --> 00:16:18,790
And you need to figure that out and go check in your monitoring how much the sessions go through return and how much not to understand where you stand there.

202
00:16:19,566 --> 00:16:24,210
It might also indicate that there is an issue if 100% of the calls go through return or 80 or 50.

203
00:16:25,070 --> 00:16:26,250
I'll ask myself why.

204
00:16:29,050 --> 00:16:29,252
Yeah.

205
00:16:29,998 --> 00:16:30,510
All right.

206
00:16:30,958 --> 00:16:33,390
So the answer is deploy and find out.

207
00:16:34,450 --> 00:16:38,050
To use a different meme.

208
00:16:38,702 --> 00:16:39,310
All right.

209
00:16:39,310 --> 00:16:40,370
So thank you, Sahi.

210
00:16:40,630 --> 00:16:42,810
Stick around for our Q&A at the end, please.

211
00:16:42,950 --> 00:16:47,570
But I'm going to switch over now to Hector, and we're going to talk about turn server deployments.

212
00:16:47,970 --> 00:16:56,650
And for those of you watching live, this is a great time to ask questions as well, to go ahead and put those in the chat, and we'll get to them by the end of the episode.

213
