So Hector, what do we need to consider when we're implementing turn servers in our WebRTC application?

214
00:17:06,089 --> 00:17:14,150
So, let me start by saying that probably the best way to manage TURN server is not having to manage them.

215
00:17:15,010 --> 00:17:24,490
I mean, unless you have a very strong reason, which they are, and I will go into that in just a couple of seconds.

216
00:17:26,253 --> 00:17:43,710
My advice would be to go with managed turn servers, because there you get the best practices, you get the distribution, you get the scaling of a mature and well-trust solution.

217
00:17:43,710 --> 00:17:56,230
because when you are turned, as Sahi mentioned, you are adding one more hop to the, to the literally to the conversation.

218
00:17:57,006 --> 00:18:17,070
And depending on what's the usage of that hop, where it's located, where are your users located, that can really impact on the user experience that you have on your WebRTC or real-time communication solution?

219
00:18:17,750 --> 00:18:22,290
So if you can show the slides, I have a couple of slides here.

220
00:18:23,290 --> 00:18:32,910
So basically, you have three ways on which you can implement TURN into your application.

221
00:18:32,910 --> 00:18:43,490
One is, well, user-managed service, using something like Fildio or Circus, which gives you the minimal maintenance.

222
00:18:43,490 --> 00:18:56,450
because you can focus only on your platform, on your solution, your application, and you can rest well knowing that you have a strong backup.

223
00:18:56,970 --> 00:19:09,330
but that is high on operational expenses, especially when you are handling a high volume of calls or connections.

224
00:19:10,158 --> 00:19:25,670
So, if you're just starting, you're not sure about what is your volume and you don't have also the expertise to manage a TURN server, the best route is going with a managed service.

225
00:19:25,710 --> 00:19:45,650
Now, there is a time where the expenses of having a managed service are higher than paying a specialized team to manage your stern and turn infrastructure.

226
00:19:45,934 --> 00:19:58,070
That's a good point to switch to a self-hosted approach in which there are basically two main ways to do this.

227
00:19:58,190 --> 00:20:13,590
One is the well-matured traditional self-hosted Kotlin server, which is probably the industry and what you will, what the managed service will probably use under the hood.

228
00:20:14,050 --> 00:20:18,190
So making the switch wouldn't be that big of a change.

229
00:20:18,830 --> 00:20:32,410
In here, you are in control over the configuration, over the cloud cost, which will be cheaper than the managed service, but it requires that manual patching piece.

230
00:20:32,570 --> 00:20:38,390
You are in charge of scaling and distribution, which is something very important that I will get deeper in a minute.

231
00:20:39,030 --> 00:20:52,150
So this allows you to manage and control your expenses, but you really need a high expertise there in order to manage this infrastructure for you.

232
00:20:52,310 --> 00:21:12,730
And there is one recent addition to this approach, which is going to the cloud native or container native by using the standard solution, which is the same benefits that you have with Kotor, but now applies to modern Kubernetes-centric approach.

233
00:21:16,622 --> 00:21:34,190
Those of you that have been working in this space for a while know that saying turn, stun and saying Kubernetes has been usually a big headache for making it work.

234
00:21:34,190 --> 00:21:52,070
but now with Stunner, this gets much easier because it's someone solving that problem and make Stun and Turn a first citizen class in the Kubernetes.

235
00:21:52,290 --> 00:22:05,510
So if you're going with or if your solution already works on the Kubernetes, then probably Stunner is a better alternative for you.

236
00:22:05,510 --> 00:22:23,330
Now, when you go to the self-hosted route, this is whether you are self-hosting using Kotor or using Stunner, there are a couple of things that you should consider regarding how to make this to work in the best way possible.

237
00:22:23,550 --> 00:22:33,730
And one thing, very basic, but a couple of teams struggle with this, is making sure the right ports are open.

238
00:22:34,270 --> 00:22:43,590
because as Sahil mentions, there are times where firewalls and networks can get a bit too restrictive.

239
00:22:43,890 --> 00:23:02,010
So making sure that you are opening the right binding ports so that you are able to communicate with the transfer server and request for an allocation.

240
00:23:02,790 --> 00:23:23,210
And this is whether if you're using pure UDP, if you're using TCP or TLS, depending on how the network is configured, you need to be aware or make sure that the right ports are open.

241
00:23:23,210 --> 00:23:33,850
And of course you cannot forget the actual relay course, which is where your media is going to be traveling.

242
00:23:34,650 --> 00:23:52,150
And the other important thing is how to keep, and probably Sandro is going to speak more about this, but making sure that the access to your TURN server is properly authenticated.

243
00:23:52,690 --> 00:24:01,668
And the recommendation is going with the share secrets with time-limited credentials.

244
00:24:02,094 --> 00:24:08,510
Code turn supports having static user and password, but that is not a good idea.

245
00:24:09,550 --> 00:24:14,210
You should stick with the time-based credentials.

246
00:24:15,190 --> 00:24:29,390
And the other important thing is having into account your global latency, making sure that your users and the turn infrastructure is closed so that you can reduce latency.

247
00:24:29,390 --> 00:24:46,610
Because if you have a user in Europe and your turn infrastructure is in the US, you are adding additional milliseconds or even seconds of the traffic traveling to the US and then to the other user.

248
00:24:46,610 --> 00:24:55,010
And there's a big clue in networking that says that the

249
00:24:57,730 --> 00:25:00,290
live speed is in the mid.

250
00:25:00,710 --> 00:25:06,330
it's a low, and media cannot go faster than that.

251
00:25:06,382 --> 00:25:19,930
so distance matters and having the ability to to have your turn infrastructure distributed closely to your users.

252
00:25:20,110 --> 00:25:20,890
It's a big thing.

253
00:25:21,730 --> 00:25:32,090
And it's one of the reasons why I advise to go with the managed services first, because these kind of providers already have that sort of infrastructure.

254
00:25:32,650 --> 00:25:40,310
And when you're self-hosting, having your big server in one central location is not enough.

255
00:25:40,710 --> 00:25:44,270
So you have to be aware of that.

256
00:25:44,490 --> 00:25:52,670
And well, since you will never know how much of your traffic is going to turn.

257
00:25:54,830 --> 00:25:59,910
Traditionally, this has been seen as an 80-20 rule.

258
00:26:01,230 --> 00:26:08,170
Now that 20% rule is more of like a baseline, because, yeah, this will depend.

259
00:26:08,170 --> 00:26:23,570
but one good starting point is guessing that at least 20% of your traffic will go to the turn infrastructure is a good starting point.

260
00:26:24,150 --> 00:26:38,596
And make sure you plan based on that regarding how you want to manage your infrastructure.

261
00:26:39,822 --> 00:26:41,930
And I think that's probably it.

262
00:26:42,810 --> 00:26:44,170
Okay, great.

263
00:26:45,934 --> 00:26:46,510
Thank you, Hector.

264
00:26:47,870 --> 00:26:50,730
We've got a question from a viewer that I'll pose to you now.

265
00:26:50,890 --> 00:26:59,170
Rafael wants to know when deploying turn servers globally to reduce latency, how do you usually handle routing users to that closest relay?

266
00:27:00,410 --> 00:27:11,600
This is the way it is managing through request and you are getting the credential, the time-based credential for that.

267
00:27:12,334 --> 00:27:26,720
You can guess based on where you get that request, what region and route that request to the closest turn server that you have there.

268
00:27:27,320 --> 00:27:38,860
You're probably going to need to have some sort of service discovery or mapping of the services that you have available and yeah, plan based on that.

269
00:27:39,950 --> 00:27:40,160
Yeah.

270
00:27:40,560 --> 00:27:47,428
And that, I mean, that gets to your first piece of advice, was if you don't have to implement your own turn server, don't do it.

271
00:27:47,520 --> 00:28:01,060
Because that example that you just gave is an example of how if you're deploying your own co-turn globally, if you've got a simple use case where maybe you don't need to think about these things, maybe, you know, maybe it's okay.

272
00:28:01,220 --> 00:28:14,320
But for most use cases where you've got a global audience, global user base across different types of networking conditions, you have to think about deploying across multi regions and load balancing between them and all of that.

273
00:28:14,894 --> 00:28:31,060
So, you know, if you have the volume to justify the expense of the DevOps team to manage that and monitor that for you, then great, that is going to be at high volume, that will be less expensive than a managed co-turn service.

274
00:28:31,400 --> 00:28:40,360
But for a lot of applications, especially in the early stages, that managed co-turn service is going to be more attractive on a cost and time investment basis.

275
00:28:40,780 --> 00:28:52,860
Yeah, same as it happens with other things like observability, for instance, or media platforms.

276
00:28:52,860 --> 00:28:58,560
These are easily a project, a different project by themselves.

277
00:28:58,940 --> 00:29:06,120
So yeah, same applies for TURN and STAN.

278
00:29:07,470 --> 00:29:07,840
Excellent.

279
00:29:08,814 --> 00:29:09,280
All right.

280
00:29:09,820 --> 00:29:10,640
Thank you, Hector.

281
00:29:10,860 --> 00:29:17,640
We're going to move over to Sandro now and talk about security concerns with TURN servers.

282
00:29:17,940 --> 00:29:24,120
So Sandro, thanks for hanging with us so long in the conversation, appreciate it.

283
00:29:24,660 --> 00:29:31,540
You gave an interesting presentation that I saw last year at the RTC conference in Krakow.

284
00:29:31,900 --> 00:29:40,360
Lots of great information on how turn servers can basically be a point of attack in your system.

285
00:29:40,560 --> 00:29:56,000
But as we've kind of discovered in a lot of situations, maybe in 20%% or maybe 50%% of the traffic in some enterprise cases, like Saki said, we really need turn servers, so we can't just turn them off for security reasons, obviously.

286
00:29:56,220 --> 00:30:02,560
So, what should teams consider in order to reduce the risk of turn servers being used for nefarious purposes?

287
00:30:04,942 --> 00:30:05,150
Yeah.

288
00:30:06,350 --> 00:30:07,210
Good question.

289
00:30:08,450 --> 00:30:10,070
So, yeah.

290
00:30:10,318 --> 00:30:16,550
I mean, we, you know, as penetration testers, because that's what we do.

291
00:30:17,110 --> 00:30:35,610
we kind of bring the hacker's mentality or hacker's view to this topic of WebRTC and especially turn servers, which is one of the more interesting areas in WebRTC security.

292
00:30:35,750 --> 00:30:36,230
in my opinion.

293
00:30:37,910 --> 00:30:47,950
And yeah, the way, you know, you guys see it as media relay, but the attacker will see it as a proxy.

294
00:30:49,390 --> 00:30:50,660
The Tern server, that is.

295
00:30:51,886 --> 00:30:55,610
Not Stun, but when we're talking about Tern specifically.

296
00:30:56,490 --> 00:30:56,510
Okay?

297
00:30:57,050 --> 00:30:58,950
And, yeah.

298
00:30:59,810 --> 00:31:05,470
Two weeks ago, we published a blog post, which is based on the talk that you mentioned.

299
00:31:06,690 --> 00:31:08,190
Thanks for sharing my screen.

300
00:31:09,250 --> 00:31:09,910
And, yeah.

301
00:31:10,750 --> 00:31:22,370
Essentially, we kind of split it up in three different attacks, or three different security threats.

302
00:31:25,810 --> 00:31:34,230
And we also published security best practices today, and a configuration guide for Kotlin.

303
00:31:34,410 --> 00:31:39,090
So yeah, there's a lot of content now all of a sudden about this very topic.

304
00:31:40,970 --> 00:31:51,570
And in terms of the first threat, I would say turn relay abuse is the number one, the most concerning.

305
00:31:54,382 --> 00:32:07,076
Security people will know what's called SSRF, server-side request forgery, where attackers can reach internal services through an API, basically.

306
00:32:07,230 --> 00:32:19,030
say cloud metadata endpoints or scan your local hosts on your servers, local host and so on and so forth, right?

307
00:32:19,210 --> 00:32:26,650
They, so, and TURN servers are a bit like that, but even better than SSRF.

308
00:32:26,702 --> 00:32:32,630
because with SSRF, you get only HTTP access, but with TURN servers.

309
00:32:32,630 --> 00:32:45,310
when you abuse TURN relay, you can get to send traffic to all the protocols, not just HTTP.

310
00:32:46,630 --> 00:32:50,710
It might be UDP and it might also be TCP sometimes.

311
00:32:51,650 --> 00:33:08,270
So turn relay abuses when an attacker uses a turn server, gets the credentials first, which are freely available in many cases because the users need those credentials.

312
00:33:10,414 --> 00:33:18,430
And then they are able to reach your internal servers that usually are not very well secured.

313
00:33:19,010 --> 00:33:24,030
So they might have weak passwords or no passwords at all, some web interfaces and so on.

314
00:33:24,110 --> 00:33:25,810
So it can be quite bad.

315
00:33:26,110 --> 00:33:32,650
And we've been finding this for a long time, actually.

316
00:33:35,086 --> 00:33:44,450
But yeah, we got bored of finding 10 relay abuse in private client engagements.

317
00:33:44,814 --> 00:33:51,350
So we turned to bug bounties so that we can actually publish about this because no one was talking about this vulnerability.

318
00:33:53,262 --> 00:34:09,550
And there's the most notable one where Slack were vulnerable and we got a bounty reward and, but more importantly for us was, you know, being able to publish this information.

319
00:34:10,638 --> 00:34:14,790
And then there were some tools on the internet which were published by other people.

320
00:34:15,406 --> 00:34:16,489
We never published ours.

321
00:34:16,942 --> 00:34:18,750
So yeah, that's one issue.

322
00:34:19,150 --> 00:34:33,449
The same relay abuse vulnerability was also mentioned last year at Hacker Conference DEF CON, which happens in Las Vegas every year.

323
00:34:33,449 --> 00:34:59,670
And the security researcher used it to pass what's called C2 traffic, which is kind of command and control traffic for controlling your botnet army, essentially, passing it through trusted services like Zoom or Microsoft Teams by using turn servers.

324
00:35:01,134 --> 00:35:04,050
So this is another way to abuse that.

325
00:35:06,310 --> 00:35:21,630
And we never really, you know, published about this because we keep finding this in our private engagements, but sometimes Stern servers are trusted, are on trusted IPs.

326
00:35:21,910 --> 00:35:29,710
they have a trusted IP address that perhaps is trusted by another system like a SIP trunk in the voice over IP world.

327
00:35:30,370 --> 00:35:38,330
And it might allow SIP toll fraud or other attacks to be conducted from the TURN server.

328
00:35:39,118 --> 00:35:48,970
So the attacker uses the TURN server to reach out to an external host that trusts the IP on the TURN server.

329
00:35:49,422 --> 00:35:56,230
And it trusts it perhaps because there is another system like a SIP server on the same IP.

330
00:35:57,710 --> 00:35:58,910
So that's one issue.

331
00:36:00,670 --> 00:36:03,510
Secondly, denial of service.

332
00:36:04,366 --> 00:36:17,430
And here we say, okay, you know, if there is the threat of direct denial of service attacks on your turn server, but how much of a problem is it?

333
00:36:17,470 --> 00:36:25,070
And we said, okay, if it's, you know, on the low digits, then maybe it's not such a big deal.

334
00:36:26,670 --> 00:36:31,710
If your turn server goes down because only that percentage of users is affected.

335
00:36:32,238 --> 00:36:38,490
It still is a problem, of course, but quite often we find out that the SFU itself is much more vulnerable.

336
00:36:38,770 --> 00:36:44,068
So, you know, one has to start prioritizing which to test and which to secure first.

337
00:36:45,838 --> 00:36:47,010
But it still is a problem.

338
00:36:47,290 --> 00:36:56,150
However, more importantly, I think, attackers last year and I suppose it's still going on.

339
00:36:56,330 --> 00:37:22,190
We don't have information if it's still going on, but last year there were a lot of turn servers becoming part of joining an army of reflection servers that reflect attacks, denial of service attacks towards the victims.

340
00:37:22,410 --> 00:37:30,930
So the Tern server can become, can reflect the traffic to victim IPs.

341
00:37:31,566 --> 00:37:44,450
when the attacker spoofs, the source IP, sends a small, small-ish, stunned message, and the Tern server then would send a larger packet to the victim.

342
00:37:45,262 --> 00:37:49,830
The victim being the one with the spoofed source IP, okay?

343
00:37:50,606 --> 00:38:07,590
Now, I ask why are turn servers being abused, because it's roughly times two, three, four, five of the original packet, while other protocols are much better at amplification.

344
00:38:08,670 --> 00:38:13,150
However, it's being done, so one has to kind of care about this.

345
00:38:13,518 --> 00:38:31,070
If you don't want to you know, receive abuse reports and join, you know, this army of turn servers, then I think you should do something about it, if you have a turn server on the internet.

346
00:38:33,166 --> 00:38:38,410
Now the third thing is turn server software vulnerabilities.

347
00:38:40,686 --> 00:38:44,330
You know, we still see a lot of unpatched Coturn servers.

348
00:38:45,614 --> 00:38:54,490
I think Coturn is the one that has had the largest fair share of vulnerabilities because it's the oldest as well.

349
00:38:55,150 --> 00:38:59,550
So, and it's written in C or C++, so, you know, there's that.

350
00:39:01,870 --> 00:39:05,630
Yeah, I mean, it's like any other software, right?

351
00:39:05,630 --> 00:39:09,150
You need to keep it up to date, otherwise it might get compromised.

352
00:39:11,070 --> 00:39:12,770
But yeah, these are the problems.

353
00:39:13,450 --> 00:39:22,370
Now, in terms of fixes, and yeah, the blog post is quite extensive.

354
00:39:22,370 --> 00:39:26,430
but in terms of fixes, we give some best practices in this article.

355
00:39:27,182 --> 00:39:31,770
but then we have published an article specifically on that.

356
00:39:31,770 --> 00:39:38,750
and we say, basically, split it in three, you know, take three approaches.

357
00:39:40,302 --> 00:39:48,650
So the first approach would be to isolate and the second one restrict and then update.

358
00:39:49,262 --> 00:39:51,396
What I mean by isolate is,

359
00:39:54,798 --> 00:40:02,630
you know, use a dedicated network or security group even for AWS terms, away from your internal systems.

360
00:40:03,430 --> 00:40:13,670
So that, you know, if someone abuses your turn server, regardless of the rules you put on it, it's kind of not so useful for the attacker.

361
00:40:14,030 --> 00:40:14,930
And that's what we found.

362
00:40:15,090 --> 00:40:23,370
In fact, when we did the bug bounty stuff, the signal app, they had a vulnerable turn server, but we couldn't reach anywhere.

363
00:40:23,770 --> 00:40:28,150
So, you know, we were like, okay, there's nothing to do here.

364
00:40:28,790 --> 00:40:32,750
Don't, you know, and they didn't, you know, they fixed it.

365
00:40:33,350 --> 00:40:35,790
But it was no big deal in their case.

366
00:40:35,890 --> 00:40:41,050
While in the case of Slack and a couple of others, it was a worse problem.

367
00:40:42,350 --> 00:40:44,830
So isolate is really useful.

368
00:40:45,690 --> 00:40:48,010
And then you should restrict, right?

369
00:40:48,090 --> 00:41:01,010
And restriction comes in different ways, but the most obvious way is to prevent relaying to internal and local IP ranges.

370
00:41:03,566 --> 00:41:09,090
And sometimes you can even restrict your relay destinations to known media servers only.

371
00:41:11,310 --> 00:41:16,990
So here we give some examples of IP ranges that you might want to block.

372
00:41:17,486 --> 00:41:31,510
Later on we have a whole table full of these IP ranges that you probably have no reason to relate to because they are not users.

373
00:41:33,070 --> 00:41:35,590
So, IPv6, don't forget about that as well.

374
00:41:38,414 --> 00:41:42,810
And then, yeah, the third one.

375
00:41:42,810 --> 00:41:58,790
And you should also limit the protocols that you allow, in the sense that you can often disable TCP relaying, for example.

376
00:42:00,366 --> 00:42:02,950
So that prevents a lot of the abuse.

377
00:42:03,170 --> 00:42:29,470
Even if you have the rules in your Codeturn server to prevent, you know, internal IP addresses from being a peer, you might still want to disable TCP relaying, for example, if your media servers are not using TCP at all, or if your users are not using TCP at all for their WebRTC traffic media.

378
00:42:30,734 --> 00:42:36,330
And there are more things, but rate limiting is, of course, really useful for various reasons.

379
00:42:39,246 --> 00:42:54,730
One thing that, you know, most of these things don't really apply to companies or software that where you're not hosting your own turn server.

380
00:42:55,694 --> 00:42:58,050
But there is one thing that does apply.

381
00:42:59,086 --> 00:43:00,290
And we mentioned here.

382
00:43:02,410 --> 00:43:06,490
which is your term credential API.

383
00:43:09,294 --> 00:43:19,350
You're probably going to take care of that yourself in your, you know, web applications to dispense these temporary time-based credentials.

384
00:43:20,974 --> 00:43:30,550
And you'll want to make sure that they, if where possible, they apply, you know, authentication and authorization.

385
00:43:31,410 --> 00:43:36,610
Now, it's not as effective as in other areas, in many cases.

386
00:43:37,810 --> 00:43:52,450
So, keep that in mind, because if you have a public facing conferencing system, you know, where anyone can register or just join anonymously, then you can't really apply authentication in the traditional sense.

387
00:43:52,590 --> 00:44:00,068
You might still be able to, you know, come up with solutions, but it would definitely not be enough.

388
00:44:01,010 --> 00:44:09,430
But yeah, that's something you have control over as even if you're using something like Twilio's hosted turn service.

389
00:44:11,310 --> 00:44:14,030
All right, and then what else do you need to do?

390
00:44:14,230 --> 00:44:23,950
So we said restrict block relay to internal local IPs, limit protocols, rate limiting, and of course, patching, right?

391
00:44:24,846 --> 00:44:25,950
Keep up to date.

392
00:44:27,050 --> 00:44:29,290
And, in fact, we just published this today.

393
00:44:30,070 --> 00:44:31,620
And where is it?

394
00:44:32,070 --> 00:44:41,770
And yesterday, Koturn actually released a new version which fixes a number of security issues.

395
00:44:43,150 --> 00:44:46,990
Incidentally, good for us for our talk here.

396
00:44:47,550 --> 00:44:49,810
And for the blog posts as well.

397
00:44:51,854 --> 00:44:58,230
So, I don't mention it here, but I think I mentioned it in the other Coturn specific article.

398
00:45:00,078 --> 00:45:13,290
One of them is the ability to bypass the access control rules by using IPv6, which we had reported in 2020, but someone found a bypass for that.

399
00:45:14,862 --> 00:45:19,610
Bypass that we had reported for the fixes that were applied.

400
00:45:21,270 --> 00:45:21,930
So yeah, there's that.

401
00:45:22,650 --> 00:45:24,230
Keep your software up to date.

402
00:45:27,630 --> 00:45:41,240
Yeah, and so yeah, we have three blog posts here that are interesting, I think, to the audience if you're running your own turn server.

403
00:45:41,480 --> 00:45:48,260
But I think I should mention that the best turn server to secure is the one you don't run.

404
00:45:49,440 --> 00:46:06,740
